{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-112455",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{2}+3(2)-10 \\\\  & =4+6-10 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{3}+8 \\\\  & =8+8 \\\\  & =16\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{3}+4(2)-16 \\\\  & =8+8-16 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $h(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
